# What goes into the source distribution.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# `python -m build` did not finish in ten minutes on this repository, and the reason is that setuptools walks the
# whole tree looking for files to include. Eleven directories here are **deliberately huge and deliberately not
# ours**: the extracted rival harness trees total about 1 GB, the vendored/alternate virtualenvs add ~300 MB, and the
# package-manager caches add more. None of it belongs in a release, and shipping it would produce an sdist larger
# than most users' disk quota for a 60k-line project.
#
# Everything excluded below is already in `.gitignore`, so a git-based sdist would skip it -- but the default
# behaviour without an explicit file is to walk the filesystem, and a build that has to stat a million files is a
# build nobody will run. This file makes the exclusion explicit and fast.
#
# ## What is INCLUDED, and why each one matters
#
# These are the non-Python files a wheel must carry or the installed package is quietly broken. All four were
# confirmed present in the built wheel rather than assumed:
#
#   * `myriaxon/builtin_skills/*/SKILL.md` -- the shipped skill. Without it `use_skill` finds nothing.
#   * `myriaxon/bench/tasks/*` -- the IQB-14 evaluation suite. Without it `myriaxon bench` has no tasks.
#   * `myriaxon/brand/assets/*.svg` -- the brand assets the dashboard serves.
#   * `py.typed` -- so a type checker treats the installed package as typed.
#
# `README.md`, `LICENSE` and `pyproject.toml` come in by default for an sdist and are listed anyway, because a
# missing LICENSE in a release is a licensing problem rather than a packaging one.

include README.md
include LICENSE
include pyproject.toml
include CHANGELOG.md
include INSTALL.md

recursive-include myriaxon/builtin_skills *.md
recursive-include myriaxon/builtin_roles *.md
recursive-include myriaxon/bench/tasks *
recursive-include myriaxon/brand/assets *.svg
include myriaxon/py.typed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Excluded. Grouped by why, so a future reader can tell a deliberate omission from an oversight.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The extracted rival harness trees. Somebody else's code, kept on disk for capability comparison and read-only.
# Reproducing the conclusions does not require carrying the trees: those live in docs/HARNESS_RESEARCH.md.
prune openclaw-src
prune opencode-dev-src
prune pi-harness-main-src
prune claude-code-main-src
prune deepseek-harness-master-src
prune redteam

# Virtualenvs. `pyproject.toml` records that `.venv-harbor-linux` needs Python 3.12 while the agent needs 3.11, so
# the two cannot share one -- but neither belongs in a release.
#
# `.uv` is 0.41 GB and was MISSING from this list. `.gitignore` excludes it, which is why it never appeared in a
# `git status`, but `MANIFEST.in` governs the sdist independently of git and setuptools walks the filesystem. It is
# uv's cache plus a downloaded CPython, so it is the same class of omission as `.npmcache` and `.pnpm-store`, which
# were added for exactly this reason. Found by listing the dot-directories at the repo root against the prune list
# rather than by trusting that the earlier sweep had caught them all.
prune .venv
prune .venv-harbor-linux
prune .tooling
prune .uv
prune .npmcache
prune .pnpm-store
prune .corepack

# Session state, caches and build output. `.myriaxon` holds a real user's transcripts and config, and a release that
# shipped somebody's conversation history would be a privacy failure rather than a packaging one.
#
# A `prune` and an `exclude` for the same name is redundant -- `prune` removes the directory and everything in it, so
# setuptools reported `warning: no previously-included files found matching '.myriaxon'` for the second line. That
# warning is the file telling us a rule does nothing, which is worth removing rather than tolerating.
prune .myriaxon
prune .git
prune .github
prune htmlcov
prune .pytest_cache
prune .ruff_cache
prune .mypy_cache
prune __pycache__

# Compiled and packaged byproducts.
#
# Written out one suffix per line. `global-exclude *.py[cod]` was the first attempt and it does nothing: `[]` is
# fnmatch syntax, not glob, and setuptools reported
# `warning: no previously-included files matching '*.py[cod]' found anywhere in distribution`. A rule that silently
# matches nothing is worse than no rule, because it reads as protection.
global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude *.pyo
global-exclude *.pyd
global-exclude *.so
global-exclude *.dll
global-exclude *.dylib
global-exclude *.zip
global-exclude *.log
global-exclude .DS_Store

# Extracted reference trees generally, so the next source drop cannot slip hundreds of megabytes into a release by
# being forgotten -- the same rule `.gitignore` uses, for the same reason.
prune *-src
